From 3ec9d9e22fe5ad11701c30efcf872cce4c948f97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timothy Schmidt <timschmidt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 01:13:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] linux: validate battery readings and retry recovered supplies

Keep failed, malformed, non-finite and out-of-range readings distinct from a real zero. Validate remaining charge with full/design data before exposing a battery, read charge fields once per refresh, and retain last good properties and update time on an unusable sample. Retry an ignored supply after usable telemetry appears, using the existing removal path to clear sibling links first.

Prefer valid capacity, then actual remaining charge, then recognized capacity levels. Preserve peripheral Unknown levels and MacBook peripheral percentages, and do not replace zero energy with an older average. Keep charger-only/design-only/relative-counter interfaces ignored and retain genuine empty-battery warnings.

Add 28 UMockdev/private D-Bus validity and lifecycle cases and build the test-only libudev NULL-read shim through Meson. The shim models ENODATA at the library boundary; it does not inject a kernel error. Base the existing simulated 1 W charge/discharge fixture on elapsed monotonic time so host scheduling does not change the simulated power.

Update the old NaN-only fixture to require no battery or warning instead of fabricated empty charge. Pace the same Wacom fixture records between main-loop intervals without changing its assertions: its event sender also fails on unchanged pinned production C, and the paced control passes there.

All 131 configured Linux tests pass with iDevice disabled. These are x86_64 mock tests; ARM packaging and native deployment remain open.
